WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.120
Well, welcome to the last
lecture for this quarter.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.640
Today, we will be
completing our roadmap.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.240
Specifically, we'll be
starting by wrapping up

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.260
our discussion on model-free
reinforcement learning first,

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.420
where we're looking at
policy optimization methods.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.860
And then we'll discuss
model-based RL.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.080
So, if you recall, basically,
what we were saying last time

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.640
is that we started looking at
policy optimization approaches

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.320
for model-free reinforcement
learning, whereby

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.920
the main idea was to basically
build and compute these policy

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.478
gradients, which are basically
the gradients of the ground

00:00:42.478 --> 00:00:44.520
truth reinforcement learning
objective that we're

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.280
interested in optimizing, which
enabled us to basically derive

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.680
the number of algorithms
ranging from reinforce

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.080
to actor-critic algorithms.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:01:00.630
And then we basically concluded
with having to basically discuss

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.750
some more recent reinforcement
learning algorithms that relate

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.870
with policy optimization.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:12.110
And if we take a step back and
think about how state-of-the-art

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.910
algorithms in machine learning
more broadly typically work,

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.870
they ultimately boil down
learning to numerical

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.070
optimization problem.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.730
So let's think
back, for example,

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:26.310
at our classical
regression problem, which

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.870
is described as a
supervised learning problem,

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.190
there we typically formulate it
as a training error minimization

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.870
problem, which we can
optimize sequentially

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.870
taking a data set of
observation until we

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.230
converge to some local optima.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.167
And this is fundamentally
different from what

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.250
we've been doing so far
in reinforcement learning.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.410
Because if we think back
at value-based methods,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.822
kind of Q-learning being
a prototypical example,

00:01:51.822 --> 00:01:54.030
this is-- well, first of
all, fundamentally different

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.090
because we're not really
optimizing an objective,

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:59.290
but we're doing fixed-point
iteration, which

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.610
can, in principle, include
all of the transitions

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.650
from an offline data set.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.890
But from the
perspective of trying

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.798
to mirror this same
formalism, this

00:02:09.798 --> 00:02:12.090
is not really optimizing the
ground truth reinforcement

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.250
learning optimization problem.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.210
On the other hand, we saw how
policy optimization methods.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.570
On one hand, do optimize for
the ground truth reinforcement

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.810
learning objective, but we
only do basically one step

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.670
of gradient descent at a time.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.210
So we saw how, at
least nominally, these

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.210
are on-policy algorithms,
where we have some interactions

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.050
with the environment, we collect
data, take one gradient step

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:35.770
and repeat.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.610
So this is really, in
a way, fundamentally

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.970
different from just positing,
this as a numerical optimization

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.330
problem and crunching
the numbers until we

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.730
converge, if that makes sense.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:50.210
So we'll discuss two specific
algorithms, namely trust region

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.450
policy optimization, TRPO for
short, and proximal policy

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.440
optimization, or PPO for short,
which originally kind of--

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.880
were born by trying
to replicate this

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.680
formalism of positing
an optimization problem

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.360
and crunching it at convergence
for the purpose of reinforcement

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.760
learning.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:10.520
Where the goal?

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.360
Therefore, will be to
define a surrogate problem

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.920
and have it such that we
can take continuous updates

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.240
to our policy before potentially
grabbing new experience

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.900
from our environment.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.520
And so we saw how in
policy optimization, what

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.480
we want to do kind
of a face value

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.800
is to compute the policy
gradient, which, last Monday, we

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.040
defined as being the expected
value of the [? grad ?]

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.600
replies multiplied by
some quantity, which is

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.460
a metric of the reward to go.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.080
We saw how an attractive
quantity to have here

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.480
is the advantage A.
Now, the first idea

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.120
is that what we
would like to have

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.230
is define this surrogate
optimization problem, whereby

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.390
if we were to take gradients of
this objective function, which

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.390
we define, the gradients
would themselves

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.350
be the policy gradient, which
are interested in following.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.590
And so a simple idea, which is
also how you might have seen

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.830
by playing with your
[? p-sets, ?] is that when you

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.550
want to introduce a concretely
implement, these kind of things,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.073
and solve them through
automatic differentiation,

00:04:20.073 --> 00:04:21.990
since we're interested,
at the end of the day,

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.930
in following this
object over here,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.710
kind of a pragmatic way of doing
so, is to define a surrogate

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.750
objective, which is a quantity
that we don't necessarily care

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.520
too much about in practice.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.770
But if we were to take the
gradient of this objective,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.850
we would actually get
the policy gradient.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.350
So in practice, this is
how we would normally

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.770
implement this through auto
differentiation methods.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.610
We will define the
surrogate objective,

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.070
and autodiff will
basically give us

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:50.650
the gradient of this object,
which is the policy gradient,

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:51.810
and we can follow that.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:05:00.070
An alternative representation
of this surrogate objective,

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.530
which we might want to
use in the exact same way,

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:08.930
is what I'm referring
here as the loss IS where

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.090
stands for importance
sampling, which is essentially

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.370
an equivalent objective to the
one shown here above, whereby

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:21.610
rather than having this defined
as the log pi's multiplied

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:25.770
by the advantage, we have this
probability density ratio which

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.910
comes from an old
version of our policy pi,

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:31.190
described by some
old parameters theta

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:35.290
old and potentially new version
of that same policy described

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.730
by some current
parameters theta.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.530
So first of all,
to sanity check,

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.330
that the gradient that
we get from these two

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.110
is equivalent just by
applying the chain rule,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.730
we will see that the--

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.250
well, the grad of the
logarithm of a function

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:54.760
is actually equal to the
gradient of this ratio.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.120
So the gradient of the function
divided by the function

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.560
evaluated at theta old.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.360
And so long story
short is that these

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.200
are two separate
surrogate functions

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.120
we could use to nominally
get the policy gradient out

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.480
of it in the same way.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.240
However, the second one, that
is the importance sampling one,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.520
has this nice interpretation
whereby this objective is

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.560
actually well-defined even
if we are using a collecting

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.620
data according to a previous
version of the policy,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.840
in this case, f
theta pi theta old.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.720
So you're probably
starting to imagine

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.600
how this form-- this objective
is still valid in the moment

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:43.400
where I'm updating my policy
multiple steps sequentially

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.060
in a row.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:48.040
So I'm kind of diverging from
my original theta old policy.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.960
And this objective is
still valid in the sense

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.460
that the importance
sampling is kind

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.940
of reweighting my
probability that I

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.540
give to the action with
respect to my previous version

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:58.860
of the policy.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.820
So I'm aware that I'm going
through this quite fast.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:08.300
But the main ideas that we
want to reach the state where

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.900
we are defining a
surrogate objective, which

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.700
still follows the main
ideas of policy gradient.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.920
Here we're seeing two
different alternatives,

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.590
which are equivalent
in that sense,

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:20.340
because these are two
loss functions which

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.200
if we differentiate, we'll
still get the policy gradient.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:25.700
But this importance
sampling version

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:27.820
has this nice
interpretation which

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.660
still holds when we are
actually considering

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.140
data collected according
to a previous version

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.500
of our same policy.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.480
Does this make sense?

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:49.210
And so, of course,
this is the objective

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.770
that is used within the trust
region policy optimization

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.495
algorithm with a caveat.

00:07:54.495 --> 00:07:56.370
And the caveat, which
we didn't discuss here,

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.090
is that-- so here
we are mentioning

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.450
this idea of taking
multiple steps

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.250
and therefore starting to
diverge from our original theta

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.210
old.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:08.630
But of course, the more
we go further away,

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.370
so the more we move from
our original theta old,

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:14.210
the more our estimate, for
example, of the advantage

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:18.730
will be less accurate,
and the more potentially

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.870
this ratio will stagnate
learning to some extent.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:25.690
And so what we want to do
is we want to potentially

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.570
take multiple steps
sequentially, as we were saying,

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:35.090
but we want to do this while
remaining within a neighborhood

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:37.613
of the original
policy within which

00:08:37.613 --> 00:08:39.530
we are kind of confident
that what we're doing

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.850
is still meaningful.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.850
And so this is the idea of
introducing what is often

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.430
referred to as a trust region.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.800
This is a pretty
common technique

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.000
in optimization, whereby given
that same objective that we were

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.800
discussing before,
we're now introducing

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.000
this constraint, which
is basically expressed

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.240
through the KL divergence,
that is a measure of divergence

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.480
between distributions.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.840
Such that, we want that the
distribution over action

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.700
induced by our old policy.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:14.040
See, the old has to be
closer with delta close

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.040
to the new policy, to the
new distribution over action

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:23.800
induced by the new policy by
theta, if that makes sense.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.303
So why is this interesting?

00:09:25.303 --> 00:09:26.720
Is because, of
course, there could

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.720
be many possible ways of
defining closeness for two

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:32.760
policies.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.000
Maybe one more
intuitive way would

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.960
be to just measure distance
in parameter space.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:41.080
For example, penalizing,
like the norm

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.120
of the distance between
the two theta vectors.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.460
But this is pretty attractive
because this is directly

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:52.820
evaluating distance in policy
distribution space, which is

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.280
ultimately what we care about.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.540
So we are measuring
distance with respect

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.640
to the distribution over actions
induced by the two policies.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.965
So maybe the parameters
vary quite a lot.

00:10:02.965 --> 00:10:04.340
But ultimately
what we care about

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.480
is that the distribution induced
by the policies remains similar.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:08.980
Yeah.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.513
Is it that there's also
expectation [INAUDIBLE]?

00:10:22.832 --> 00:10:23.760
Yeah.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.420
So this depends also
if you're averaging

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:29.760
over different trajectories.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.220
So remember that this will be
doing an empirical evaluation

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:33.720
of this.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.060
So we'll be sampling multiple
trajectories over time

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.000
and doing empirical means
instead of expectations,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:39.900
but this is why.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:40.400
Yeah.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:51.490
So this was incredibly
successful at the time, meaning,

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:55.210
it was able to be a pretty
reliable reinforcement learning

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.410
algorithm, especially for
continuous control problems.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.010
However, it came with its
own set of limitations

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:04.810
because, of course,
now we are changing

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:07.050
from having to solve an
unconstrained problem

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.010
to actually having a constraint
in our formulation, which

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.770
complicates things.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.610
Specifically, in order
to solve these problems,

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.530
people usually use conjugate
gradient methods, which

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.730
can be tricky to implement.

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:27.170
And empirically, this
algorithm usually

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.890
seemed to perform
pretty weakly when

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:32.270
we're using maybe
deep neural networks,

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.690
such as convolutional neural
networks or large transformer

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:40.290
networks, which was indeed
one of the main practical

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.120
limitations of TRPO.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.680
So this algorithm was
still extremely impactful

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:52.360
because, in a way, it motivated
successive algorithms which

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:56.000
try to simplify and remove
these limitations, which is

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.820
exactly what PPO actually is.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:01.400
So PPO in a way is a
derivative of TRPO,

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.200
which is trying fundamentally
to answer the question of,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.640
can we replicate the
exact same objective

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.480
that we were trying to achieve
with TRPO without having

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.140
to resort to this second
order optimization,

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:16.960
which is basically entailing
this more complex optimization

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.840
problem that we have to solve
in the constrained problem

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.880
that we introduced in
the previous slide.

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:26.500
Is this setting clear on the
transition from TRPO to PPO?

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:36.280
And so, if you look around
for different implementations

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.920
of PPO, most likely
you will stumble

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.360
upon one of these two versions.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.197
So the first one is, well, we
have this constraint problem.

00:12:44.197 --> 00:12:45.780
We are saying that
this is introducing

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:49.180
some challenges, at least from
an implementation perspective.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.700
Let's basically
move the constraint

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.180
to the objective
function with a penalty

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.580
through Lagrange
multipliers, which then leads

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.140
to an unconstrained problem.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:02.420
And let's solve this through
stochastic gradient descent

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.100
tools, as we have always done.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.900
So in a way, this
is completely valid.

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:11.820
Although, I would say that
the most popular version is

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:16.420
this second version here,
where we're basically

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:21.820
trying to empirically replicate
the behavior of the constraint

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:27.500
through an empirically
derived equation in a way.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:32.540
So we are basically introducing
this specific objective here,

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:36.280
where we are trying to replicate
the behavior of this constraint,

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.340
which we introduced before.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:39.530
How are we doing so?

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:43.010
We're doing so by, first of
all, defining the ratio just

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:46.170
for compactness as
a small r of theta.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.490
So this is the ratio between
the two policy distributions

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:50.970
which we're using before.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:56.610
Of course, if we evaluate r at
the old version of the parameter

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.370
theta, we will
get r equals to 1.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.410
And the objective is
comprised as the minimization

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:04.250
between two terms.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.850
The minimization
here is used as such

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:11.650
that we are basically having
that this overall objective is

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.490
a lower bound of
the ground truth

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.627
objective of the ratio
times the advantage.

00:14:16.627 --> 00:14:18.210
So we're basically
picking the minimum

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.730
between the ratio
times the advantage,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:23.810
and this clip term over here.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:27.290
So what does the
clip function do?

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:32.850
We're basically taking
the ratio, small r,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.412
assume this is the axis where
we are considering small r.

00:14:36.412 --> 00:14:37.870
And we are basically
saying that we

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.990
are constraining it to be
between 1 minus epsilon

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.750
and 1 plus epsilon.

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:50.430
So if r falls within these two
bounds, we are keeping the--

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.150
well, the value of r.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:56.450
Otherwise, we are clipping
it to r theta would be here,

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.590
we would be using
1 minus epsilon.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.270
If r theta would be greater
than 1 plus epsilon,

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:02.770
we would be using
1 plus epsilon.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:04.990
So we're basically
keeping r theta

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:07.190
in between these values,
and then the extremes

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:08.690
when we're going
beyond that value.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:14.450
Of course, this gets then
multiplied by the advantage.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.070
So you're starting
to see how this

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:20.310
is, at least intuitively, an
empirical way in which you can

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:25.590
somehow approximate this idea
whereby we want to stay closer

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:30.990
to the original policy, because
we're basically these values

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:36.180
are constraining the ratio
r theta to be only as kind

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:39.700
of not incredibly different from
the original policy, if that

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:40.320
makes sense.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:45.380
Yeah.

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:46.760
What is better?

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.980
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:02.780
So the point of the
minimum here is that--

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:06.620
so the full picture
here is that we

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:10.660
are interested in the gradients
that we get from this objective.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:14.340
And specifically, we want
to saturate the gradients

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:18.780
whenever we are beyond
these clip values,

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:23.080
but only if these are
in a negative direction.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:26.680
Meaning, if we are moving
very far away from the policy,

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:29.220
but we are moving
in a direction that

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:31.700
is negative for performance,
we want to clip it.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:35.650
On the other hand, if
taking a bigger step

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:43.230
would allow us to bring us
closer to the previous policy,

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:45.410
we are still enabling
gradients to flow.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:47.410
So this minimum--

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.250
I advise you to maybe
even just plot this

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.287
and trying to see case
by case scenarios.

00:16:52.287 --> 00:16:54.370
But the overall intuition,
I think, there are two.

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:57.570
One, this allows us
to have a lower bound

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:58.970
on the actual objective.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:00.570
And two, if you
look at the behavior

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.310
of the resulting gradients that
you get from this objective,

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:05.609
you're getting something
that saturates whenever

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:07.530
it is actually good
to saturate, and it

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.849
doesn't when you want
to recover, essentially.

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:10.349
Yeah.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:14.390
But yeah.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:18.490
So long story short, PPO is
derived from the same intuitions

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:21.450
that we were discussing
before from TRPO.

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:23.609
And this is definitely
the most popular version

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:25.030
you will find out there.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:27.490
And I would arguably say
that now PPO is probably

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:30.850
one of the most, if not the most
popular reinforcement learning

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:32.970
algorithm out there.

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:40.570
So before moving
to today's lecture,

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:42.530
first of all, any
questions so far?

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:50.710
So I want to spend just a couple
of minutes really driving home

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:52.270
the main messages
which we discussed

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:55.110
so far for model-free RL.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:58.030
And so remember how we basically
started all of our discussions

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:01.430
a few weeks back by talking
about different ways

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.310
to estimate value functions,
where we were originally

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:06.070
motivated by the
appealing properties

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:10.190
of dynamic programming,
where these were essentially

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:18.110
allowing us to decompose
the problem over time

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:22.870
by enabling us to have an exact
representation of the value

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:25.830
for each state at the
end of our problem

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:29.150
by just looking at the
one step propagation.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.620
And we were able to do this
because we are actually

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.140
able to compute exactly
those expectations

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:37.420
through the use of the knowledge
of the system dynamics.

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:40.300
And so saw how these methods
were in principle exact

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:44.060
because we were not losing any
information through this one

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.460
step expectation,
but it required

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.540
us to have a knowledge
of the underlying MDP.

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:51.140
And so this motivated
to introduce

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.900
two fundamental model-free
learning paradigms, Monte Carlo

00:18:54.900 --> 00:19:00.258
learning, which basically used
sampling from our environment.

00:19:00.258 --> 00:19:02.300
In other words, interactions
with our environment

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:06.220
to approximate that expectation
and specifically define

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:09.260
the update to our value
function as taking a step

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.655
in the direction of
the observed returns.

00:19:11.655 --> 00:19:13.280
So we would sample
in our environments,

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:15.640
reach a terminal state,
observe a return,

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:17.260
and update our
estimate of the value

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.300
function towards that return.

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.600
On one hand, this is great
because it's unbiased.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.460
This is an actual
unbiased estimator

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.580
of the ground truth return.

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:28.500
But it is high
variance because there

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:31.610
is a lot of stochastic
sampling happening over time,

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:35.010
and we need to reach a
terminal state in order

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.490
to actually sum backwards
all of the rewards

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.810
that we have obtained.

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:41.970
On the other hand, temporal
difference learning

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:45.713
tries to combine ideas from both
of these paradigms, the sampling

00:19:45.713 --> 00:19:47.130
of Monte Carlo
because we're still

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:48.930
interacting with
the environment,

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:51.230
and the bootstrapping
of dynamic programming

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:55.730
because we are updating our
guess towards the one step ahead

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:59.450
estimate of our future return.

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:01.570
Which is great, because
this lowers the variance

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:04.130
because we're sampling only
once, instead of having

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.130
to account for all
the stochasticity

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:09.290
of the possible futures
and it can learn online,

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:13.770
because we can do this update
with just a single step.

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:16.610
But it does introduce bias
because we are updating

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:18.210
the [INAUDIBLE] I guess.

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:21.530
And so there are
some trade-offs.

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:24.730
Of course, we mentioned how this
is true both for state value

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:27.370
functions V, but
also for Q functions,

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:29.050
and this holds equally.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:35.310
The other main idea is that
we saw how all of these ideas

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:39.290
basically held true for the
tabular representations,

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:42.690
whereby value functions
are just lists or matrices,

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.790
and each entry in
those lists of matrices

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:49.710
is our estimate for
the value function.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:53.450
And it holds similarly for
value function approximation.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:56.130
Whereby rather than
having lists or matrices,

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.630
we have a parametric
function described

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:01.590
by some parameters theta,
which we can basically

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:04.590
tune in order to better
approximate these values.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.230
How do we tune them?

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:09.990
Exactly how we were defining
before these backups

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:11.490
for the tabular case.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.750
Now the same targets, that is
the return and the TD target

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:20.090
represent elements in a
update for in parameter space.

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:24.910
With all of these
machinery, we then

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.860
looked at value-based methods
and policy optimization methods

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:31.140
as two broad families of
model-free reinforcement

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.020
learning approaches.

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:36.300
In value-based methods, we used
generalized policy iteration

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:41.620
as the fundamental scheme in
order to obtain optimal policy.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:44.680
In generalized policy
iteration, loosely speaking,

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:50.020
alternates between two
processes, a policy evaluation

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:52.700
step, whereby giving
a policy, we estimate

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:54.420
the value for the policy.

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:56.840
And the policy improvement
step, which on the other hand,

00:21:56.840 --> 00:22:00.020
tries to be greedy with respect
to the newly updated value

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.100
function.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:04.560
And we saw on-policy and
off-policy algorithms,

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:07.440
namely SARSA and Q-learning
as specific examples,

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:08.860
and how these were
instantiations

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.260
of that same framework.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:13.820
Of course, this
applies also when

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.000
you have deep neural
nets in the picture,

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:21.540
and we looked at deep neural
networks as a specific example.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.570
For policy optimization,
which we just concluded today,

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:28.330
we, on the other hand, said
that in policy optimization

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:31.730
we care about learning an
explicit parametric policy theta

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:34.690
pi theta with some
parameters theta.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:39.010
And we want to optimize these
parameters to directly optimize

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:42.890
the reinforcement learning
objective, as we said today.

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:46.230
And this at a very high
level entails well,

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:47.650
estimating the
gradient if we want

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:50.230
to solve them through
gradient descent techniques.

00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:52.650
And so all of our discussions
of policy gradient

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.978
fall into step one here.

00:22:54.978 --> 00:22:56.770
And then we want to do
approximate gradient

00:22:56.770 --> 00:22:59.770
descent using this gradient.

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:02.050
We saw how policy
gradient naturally

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:03.970
describes this idea
of trial and error

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:07.650
learning by essentially
being a weighted version

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.830
of the maximum
likelihood gradient.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.010
If you recall here, we
were describing this idea

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:16.250
where the policy gradient
tries to basically push up

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:22.370
the probability assigned to
actions which have high reward.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:25.890
And so this encodes this idea
of trial and error learning.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:32.710
But we saw how policy gradient
is fundamentally doomed

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:35.343
by this problem of high
variance of the gradient itself.

00:23:35.343 --> 00:23:37.510
And so we discussed a number
of different strategies

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:38.730
to reduce the variance.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.790
Namely, we looked
at baselines and we

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:43.710
introduced critics in
actor-critic methods

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:47.190
and so forth.

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:50.097
So if you wish this is like a
3-minute run through everything

00:23:50.097 --> 00:23:51.930
we've been doing over
the last few lectures.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.670
But I think it's good
to make sure that we are

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:56.090
driving the main ideas home.

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:57.730
But any questions on this?

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:13.350
So remember that basically we
were introducing this skeleton,

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:16.870
and all of the methods we
discussed so far, essentially

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:20.610
are different instantiation
of this key RL skeleton.

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:24.900
So we always have this
generate samples step where we

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:26.660
interact with an environment.

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:31.020
We typically could estimate
a return or a value function

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.780
of some sort, so we could
be having a value function

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.280
approximator, for example.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:38.580
We'll see today how we
could fit the model as well.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:41.260
And then we could use whatever
we do in the second step

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:45.180
to improve the policy, either
through a greedy policy

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:46.980
improvement, as we're
doing in Q-learning,

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.020
or through gradient
descent, as we're doing

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:52.220
in policy optimization methods.

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:56.360
And at the beginning of our
reinforcement learning lectures,

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:58.900
we briefly mentioned
a number of trade-offs

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:02.900
between algorithms and ways
to navigate these algorithms.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:06.100
But I hope that maybe before
these trade-offs were,

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.780
I guess, a little bit vague.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:10.480
But now if we actually look
at those same slides again,

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:13.420
I hope that most of these
trade-offs start to make sense.

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:16.580
So we're discussing trade-offs
in terms of sample efficiency

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:17.520
and stability.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:20.800
And so I think here on-policy
and off-policy algorithms are

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:23.680
exactly hinting at these
trade-offs over here,

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:26.320
that is, how much
is it expensive

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:29.920
for us to interact
with the environment?

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:33.840
We looked at continuous
or discrete action spaces,

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:38.320
which could have implications in
choosing value-based methods as

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.720
opposed to policy
optimization methods.

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:42.920
We looked at episodic
or infinite horizon

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:46.840
cases, where Monte Carlo
requires an episodic setting,

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:52.240
because we need a terminal state
to back propagate those values.

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:54.080
And loosely speaking,
I think we started

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:59.060
to explore this spectrum
of sample efficiency.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.940
So we were looking
at, for example,

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:04.520
ranging from the less
efficient algorithms,

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:07.360
all the way here we see
on-policy, policy gradient

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:08.260
algorithms.

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:10.400
We have actor-critic
algorithms, which

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:14.240
try to leverage value function
approximation to become

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.840
more sample efficient.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.960
We looked at
off-policy Q-learning

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:20.990
as example, where we could use
offline data or data gathered

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:23.810
from other policies, which
makes it more sample efficient.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:27.110
And today we'll be
introducing some key ideas

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:29.950
in the realm of model-based RL.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:34.950
But long story short, is that
we introduced these trade-offs,

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:37.590
I guess, pretty vaguely
in the first lecture.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:39.190
But hopefully
you're appreciating

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:42.090
how many of these trade-offs
now are more concrete.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:51.190
And so let's move to
model-based approaches.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:55.850
Specifically today, we'll
be starting from-- well,

00:26:55.850 --> 00:27:00.950
introducing a basic recipe
for model-based reinforcement

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:02.270
learning.

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:06.350
And we'll discuss the key
limitations and challenges that

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.463
come from this basic recipe.

00:27:09.463 --> 00:27:11.630
And then we'll start to see
basically different ways

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:15.150
of addressing those challenges
and limitations, namely

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:17.440
through the lens of
uncertainty quantification.

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:20.740
And so we'll have a small
parenthesis on uncertainty

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:22.700
quantification in general.

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:25.620
And then we'll conclude with
some examples, as well as

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:31.620
some more broader scope
remarks for the entire class.

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.900
The caveat here is
that, of course,

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:38.380
model-based RL is goes
clearly beyond anything

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.300
that we'll be discussing today.

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:42.520
But for today, I hope
that at the very least,

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:45.100
we'll be conveying the main
ideas and challenges that

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:47.760
come through model-based
reinforcement learning.

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:54.540
So let's start from introducing
this general recipe.

00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:58.960
So at the very kind
of fundamental level,

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:02.340
if we knew the dynamics, that
is a function that basically

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.860
predicts the next state,
given the state and an action

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:08.220
that we take in that
state, we could use tools

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.580
from optimal control.

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:12.220
So basically pick a
method from the first half

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:13.520
of this class we could use.

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:15.580
We have a model, we
could plan through it,

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:18.360
we could use a direct
method, indirect method.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:20.640
Basically, our favorite choice.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.480
And so a very simple
idea would be,

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:25.880
well, we want this
object because it

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:29.600
could allow us to use any of
the tools we discussed before.

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.360
Let's use interactions
with the environment

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:36.800
to collect data and examples
of these transitions and let's

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:40.380
learn an approximate model of
this ground truth dynamics.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.040
And so we could
apply a strategy that

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:49.000
looks like the one shown
here on the slide, whereby

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:53.660
we might have a base
policy pi 0, in this case.

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:54.540
This could be random.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:56.400
It could be an
exploration policy

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:59.360
that is given to us from some
domain knowledge or anything

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:00.800
that we choose.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:02.822
And we could
collect and we would

00:29:02.822 --> 00:29:04.280
run this in the
environment and use

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:08.480
this to collect a data set of
transitions, which are basically

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:11.880
comprised of these
triplets over here.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:17.790
So we would be running our
policy in the environment.

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:21.150
In doing so, we would be seeing
examples of these one step

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:26.190
transitions, which we can
then collect to basically

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:31.350
be examples of mapping
from state and control

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:34.270
to a next state.

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:38.150
Once we have this data set, we
can then fit a dynamical model

00:29:38.150 --> 00:29:41.790
to this data through our
well, favorite regression

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:44.350
or supervised
learning technique.

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:46.910
For example, minimizing
some form of squared error

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.870
or maximizing some log
likelihood of our data.

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:54.470
And then we could use this
model to basically plan.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:57.550
So the question here
is, would this work?

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:59.970
Well, in some cases, yes.

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:05.010
So especially if dynamics are
relatively simple, for example,

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:07.990
we could have some linear
time invariant dynamics.

00:30:07.990 --> 00:30:10.430
This tends to work pretty well.

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:14.420
And this also relates to some
of the discussions we were doing

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:17.500
on system
identification, whereby

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.700
if you have a fairly
well-understood model,

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:24.820
if the underlying dynamics
are within the function class

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:26.740
that we're trying to
fit and maybe we only

00:30:26.740 --> 00:30:30.660
have to tune a limited
number of parameters,

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.820
then these tools
actually work quite well,

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:38.440
and we might hope to generalize
good enough to the real system.

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:45.940
Of course, this doesn't hold in
probably the majority of cases,

00:30:45.940 --> 00:30:48.740
specifically when we're dealing
with complex, potentially

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:51.460
nonlinear dynamics,
and especially

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.820
in the combination
of complex dynamics,

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:56.920
and high capacity
approximate models,

00:30:56.920 --> 00:30:59.020
for example, neural networks.

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:02.900
And this is because
extrapolating from observed data

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:05.500
to, for example,
out-of-distribution examples

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:09.220
from our environment is
difficult and can be misleading.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:11.380
So for basically the
rest of this class,

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:16.360
we'll be looking at ways
to basically or approaches

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:20.440
to try and have this exact same
formalism work in the cases

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:25.540
where we might be having
complex dynamics to learn.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:33.620
So why is this an issue?

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:38.400
So let us look at a fictitious
and a pictorial example.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:40.540
So say we are in this
very simple world,

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:42.500
the agent is the black
dot here on the slide,

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:45.920
and we have an obstacle
which we wish to avoid.

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:49.640
The goal here is to go as
further north as possible.

00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:52.520
So in this case, say that
our exploration policy pi

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:56.960
0 is something that explores a
certain amount, certain subset

00:31:56.960 --> 00:32:01.000
of the state space, specifically
describing the state

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.040
distribution under
policy pi 0 P sub pi

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:06.840
0 of x, which is basically
the subset of the state

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:10.470
space induced by that policy.

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:12.310
Of course, if we
were to learn now

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:15.630
a dynamical model on this
transitions, which we collected

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:19.270
through this experience,
we might-- and then

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:21.070
we use this for
planning, we might

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:25.430
be ending doing something
extremely meaningful

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:28.928
within the realm or the
states that we have observed.

00:32:28.928 --> 00:32:30.470
But the moment where
we change and we

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:34.510
go outside of the state
distribution we have covered,

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:36.270
this is obviously not the case.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:38.750
And so if you remember
also our discussions

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:42.070
on imitation learning,
this is another example

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:46.150
of covariate shift and
distribution shift,

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:50.750
whereby basically, when we use
the learned dynamical model

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:53.690
as an inner component of
our planning algorithm,

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:57.790
we're then observing a
different state distribution.

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:00.030
And so here generalization
and extrapolation

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.450
across these settings is
actually what matters.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:13.530
So what could be first fairly
heuristic and empirical way

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:18.170
to improve the basic recipe
we were describing before?

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:20.690
So again, let's consider
our three steps where

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:23.610
we take our policy, run
it in the environment,

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:27.310
collect some data, use
this data to fit a model,

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:29.890
and then say that we
want to use this to plan.

00:33:29.890 --> 00:33:31.990
Of course, what we
could do, first of all,

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:35.850
is to plan in a receding
horizon fashion.

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:41.490
So we know that the plan might
be slightly off or at least

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:44.470
might be accurate closer
to our initial time,

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:48.570
but then we might want to
reevaluate where we actually are

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.270
in the environment and replan.

00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:53.610
And so in this case, using
a model predictive control

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.770
approach might be beneficial.

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:59.770
So this is a simple
improvement here.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:03.410
But also what we could
do is we could basically

00:34:03.410 --> 00:34:06.120
use the transitions
that we are observing

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:09.600
while deploying this policy to
basically add them to the data

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.340
set and refit our model.

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:14.679
So in a way, we are
gradually closing

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:17.679
the gap between the state
distribution induced

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:21.679
by the original policy and
the state distribution induced

00:34:21.679 --> 00:34:24.179
by this planning policy,
if that makes sense.

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:33.520
So these are indeed
two simple steps

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:39.960
which can in practice make
a substantial improvement.

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.000
However, I would
say the main focus

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:44.900
for today is on
uncertainty quantification.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:49.199
And we'll see-- we'll
see why shortly.

00:34:49.199 --> 00:34:57.840
Specifically, the reason
why these techniques fail

00:34:57.840 --> 00:35:01.500
is that in theory, what we
would like to have is-- well,

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:03.920
we have a very complex
dynamical model or system

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:05.750
we're interacting
with, and we would

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:08.230
like to have a high
capacity model that

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:11.150
is able to represent
these complex dynamics

00:35:11.150 --> 00:35:12.330
in the high data regime.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.950
So we want to have
high capacity models.

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:17.310
However, we also want
this high capacity model

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:20.070
to not overfit to
the observed data

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:23.990
if we only have a
limited set of that data.

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:27.190
The reason why we don't want
this overfitting problem

00:35:27.190 --> 00:35:33.230
is because if we then use
an optimizer or a planning

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:35.310
algorithm, for example,
as we were doing

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:39.750
in our previous recipe to
optimize over our learned model,

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:45.910
the optimization will be
prone to exploiting errors

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:49.230
in the positive direction,
if that makes sense.

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:55.710
So visually, say that we
have only a limited number

00:35:55.710 --> 00:36:01.870
of data points represented by
the red dots here on the slide,

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:06.010
here we have two equivalent
functions that fit the data.

00:36:06.010 --> 00:36:08.330
But the blue one
here is potentially

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:11.090
representing a higher
capacity model that

00:36:11.090 --> 00:36:12.370
is overfitting to the data.

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:14.970
So it's fitting perfectly
our observed data,

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:20.410
but then it is doing-- it
is behaving quite wildly

00:36:20.410 --> 00:36:23.090
outside of the observed data.

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:25.210
Now, say that you
are an optimizer that

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:29.070
wants to maximize the
performance from the blue model,

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:33.770
you might be tempted to choose
behavior that basically leads

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:36.130
to exploiting the
positive reward

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:37.910
predictions under our model.

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:41.870
And so I think this
is a key problem here

00:36:41.870 --> 00:36:43.730
and a key tension
when you want to use

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:46.570
learned models on one hand
that might not extrapolate

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:50.090
or generalize well with
an optimization that

00:36:50.090 --> 00:36:53.650
is in a way acting as
a black box optimizer

00:36:53.650 --> 00:36:56.270
and could exploit errors
in the positive direction.

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:04.280
So for today, what
we will be looking

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:08.120
at is a possible solution to
this problem, which is basically

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:13.040
considering uncertainty and
loosely speaking, how certain

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:16.080
our model is about its
prediction as a mechanism

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:21.700
to basically push against
these possible flaws.

00:37:25.585 --> 00:37:26.460
Does that make sense?

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.700
Any questions on this?

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:39.300
So first of all, we're talking
about uncertainty estimation

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:43.320
and what do we mean by
uncertainty on the predictions

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:44.480
of our model.

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:47.680
And specifically, what
we mean by uncertainty

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:51.280
is we mean an expression
of a distribution

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:53.320
over possible outcomes.

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:58.040
So for example, instead of
predicting a single point

00:37:58.040 --> 00:38:02.350
estimate of what is the
most likely next best

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:05.230
state from given a
state and a control,

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:09.390
we want our system
models to predict

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:12.510
a full distribution and
associated likelihood

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:15.290
of what is most likely
to happen in the future,

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:16.130
if that makes sense.

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:18.870
So visually, what we want
from our dynamical model

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:22.710
is something that gives us a
range of possible scenarios

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:28.750
that we could be optimizing for
with the associated probability.

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:30.310
We want this because
this essentially

00:38:30.310 --> 00:38:34.410
allows us to reason in terms of
expectations under our model.

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:38.990
So why is this--

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:41.790
why can this really
a form planning?

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:44.710
So consider again
this simple example,

00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:48.310
where we are the black
dot on the slide.

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:51.830
We want to reach a goal state
represented by the green star,

00:38:51.830 --> 00:38:56.430
and we have this cliff-like
environment where the lines here

00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:58.230
represent the edge of the cliff.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:02.730
So if we go beyond this,
episode terminates essentially.

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:06.210
Now, say that we have
two candidate open loop

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:07.170
trajectories.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:10.690
So we basically
assume we have access

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:23.310
to U1 to UT prime and U1 double
prime to UT double prime.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:28.890
And say we want to use
something along the lines

00:39:28.890 --> 00:39:33.610
of the model-based algorithms
we're using before, we would

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:36.730
basically be
simulating in our head

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:39.570
these two possible action
trajectories, open loop

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:41.970
trajectories, where
one would lead

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:45.570
us to very close to the
goal and one would lead us

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:47.650
a little bit more further away.

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:49.850
In this case, the
circles represent

00:39:49.850 --> 00:39:54.450
our estimate of how certain we
are about where we would end up.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:56.350
In this case, both.

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:58.800
In both cases, we are
fairly certain about where

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.840
we are ending up,
and so it is clear

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:04.000
that the trajectory associated
with the yellow circle

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:09.320
is better because it allows
us to be closer to the goal.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:14.800
However, despite having in a
second case assumed that now we

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:18.600
have broader uncertainty bands
around our terminal point,

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:22.800
despite the terminal the
best-- the expected value

00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:25.080
being the same in
both cases, now we

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:27.280
have a greater
uncertainty over those.

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:32.040
And so the expected reward under
this higher variance prediction

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:34.600
would be lower for
the yellow circle.

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:37.700
Because under this
new uncertainty bound,

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:39.200
we are actually
considering the case

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:41.680
that in actually the
majority of the cases

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:45.340
we are ending up off the
cliff, if that makes sense.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:51.840
So the reason why we care
about uncertainty estimation

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:54.980
is because it assuming
we are able to do this.

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:56.630
So we're able to
learn a model that

00:40:56.630 --> 00:40:58.910
gives us an estimate
of how certain

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:01.070
we are about our prediction.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:04.030
It gives us a mechanism
to express performance

00:41:04.030 --> 00:41:07.490
as expected reward
under our model.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:25.210
Any questions here?

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:25.710
Cool.

00:41:28.890 --> 00:41:32.170
So the next question is, how
can we model uncertainty?

00:41:32.170 --> 00:41:33.910
We say we want to do this.

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:36.190
What are different
ways of doing so?

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:39.910
So the first idea is
we could use the output

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:43.790
entropy from our given model.

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:47.430
So again, pictorially,
what we are trying to do

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:50.350
is we are trying to learn a
parametric function, which

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:56.250
maps from states and controls to
probabilities over next state.

00:41:56.250 --> 00:42:00.730
In order to do this, we
might be fitting, either--

00:42:00.730 --> 00:42:03.910
depending on whether the state
space is discrete or continuous,

00:42:03.910 --> 00:42:06.530
we might be fitting either
a categorical distribution

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:07.550
over next states.

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:09.650
So we're assigning a
probability to each

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:11.610
of our possible next states.

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:15.130
Or we might be fitting a
continuous distribution, say

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:18.090
a Gaussian, which
basically expresses

00:42:18.090 --> 00:42:20.530
the mean value of
where we expect to be

00:42:20.530 --> 00:42:25.690
and our standard deviation and
the variance of our uncertainty.

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:28.770
In this case, of
course, we could

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:31.970
use the entropy that basically
comes from the definition

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:33.590
of the distribution itself.

00:42:33.590 --> 00:42:36.130
For example, we might be
using the standard deviation

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:37.710
of the fitted Gaussian.

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:47.970
However, model entropy
is different from what

00:42:47.970 --> 00:42:51.700
is often referred in literature
as epistemic uncertainty.

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:54.200
So you might have heard about
this distinction before, which

00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:57.460
is basically the distinction
between aleatoric uncertainty,

00:42:57.460 --> 00:43:01.320
that is the noise that
is intrinsically related

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:04.360
with the process that
we are observing.

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:09.840
For example, here on the slide,
we see this parabolic process.

00:43:09.840 --> 00:43:12.360
We have some observations
of this process,

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:15.400
and there is some
inherent noise that might

00:43:15.400 --> 00:43:18.960
be observed from this process.

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:21.940
The other type of uncertainty
is epistemic uncertainty,

00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:25.880
whereby we are actually
uncertain about the model

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:26.920
itself.

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:29.340
So here we were certain
about, for example,

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:32.007
the specific parameters
of this parabola.

00:43:32.007 --> 00:43:33.840
It's just there was
some inherent noise that

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:36.600
is impossible to model.

00:43:36.600 --> 00:43:41.080
On the other hand, again, using
this kind of fictitious example

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:46.060
over here, we might have two
possible alternative models,

00:43:46.060 --> 00:43:48.240
the green one and
the blue one, which

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.820
with associated different
parameters, which

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:53.940
fit equally the model, the
observed data, but are just

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.260
two different models.

00:43:56.260 --> 00:43:58.300
So here we will have
two different values

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:00.340
of the parameters theta
that describe these two

00:44:00.340 --> 00:44:01.300
different curves.

00:44:01.300 --> 00:44:05.400
And so this is an uncertainty
between on the model itself.

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:08.380
So which of these two
realizations of my parameters

00:44:08.380 --> 00:44:13.680
is actually the one that is
better representing my data.

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:24.320
So using the entropy gets
us some way along this path.

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:29.040
But it clearly does not account
for what we actually care about,

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:33.060
which is the
potentially mutually

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:36.260
coexistent and alternative
explanations of the data

00:44:36.260 --> 00:44:37.840
according to different models.

00:44:46.340 --> 00:44:49.730
So how do we model for this--

00:44:49.730 --> 00:44:52.370
how do we estimate for
this model uncertainty?

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:56.170
I would say probably
the most common approach

00:44:56.170 --> 00:44:59.330
for doing so is leveraging
Bayesian statistics

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:05.610
and Bayesian approaches,
whereby typically what we are

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:07.890
interested in
doing with Bayesian

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:12.250
approaches is that we want to
basically express this model

00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:16.330
uncertainty as a distribution
over parameter values

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:20.090
theta given the observed data.

00:45:20.090 --> 00:45:24.610
So until now, when we were
discussing fitting a model

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:26.770
to our data, we're
often just saying

00:45:26.770 --> 00:45:31.010
that we would be converging
to a specific value

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:33.450
of our parameters theta.

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:36.810
On the other hand, if we are
uncertain about the model

00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:39.370
itself, we might be
interested in modeling

00:45:39.370 --> 00:45:41.890
maybe a full distribution
of how certain

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:47.400
we are about what the real value
of the parameters theta is.

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:51.840
And so this is what we mean by
the distribution over parameters

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:53.998
given the data set.

00:45:53.998 --> 00:45:55.540
That is we are given
some observation

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:58.760
and we want to ideally
model the full distribution

00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:01.100
of plausible hypothesis.

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:06.880
Why is this useful?

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:10.200
Is because once we have
this full distribution,

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:14.720
we could actually predict
through our learned dynamical

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:18.080
model by basically
averaging out all

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:20.040
the possible future
predictions that

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:22.880
would happen if we were
to basically average out

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.360
over this full distribution.

00:46:25.360 --> 00:46:29.160
And so this is referred to
as the predictive posterior

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:30.140
distribution.

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:37.575
Formally, this is expressed
through this integral over here

00:46:37.575 --> 00:46:39.200
where we're basically
saying that we're

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:42.760
computing the prediction
of our next state averaged

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:48.660
over all possible values
of my parameters theta.

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:52.460
I think as often the
case, to visualize

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.060
these integrals is
often useful to see

00:46:55.060 --> 00:46:56.680
this in the discrete setting.

00:46:56.680 --> 00:47:00.892
So say we have only two values
of our parameters theta.

00:47:00.892 --> 00:47:03.100
This integral, this predictive
posterior distribution

00:47:03.100 --> 00:47:06.540
would essentially entail
running my predictions

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:09.620
for both of my values
of theta, and then

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:11.820
weighting the prediction
based on the probability

00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:14.060
of each individual theta.

00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:18.200
This is the continuous
expansion of that same idea.

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:23.620
How does this look in practice?

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:26.800
Well, of course, we won't
have time to cover this here.

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:29.980
But Bayesian statistics
basically provides

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:32.940
and the Bayes' theorem
provides basically a mechanism

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:36.220
to retrieve the posterior
distribution over parameters

00:47:36.220 --> 00:47:38.860
from data.

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:42.140
But let's look at what
this means in practice.

00:47:42.140 --> 00:47:46.290
For example, say we want to
solve a regression problem where

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:48.770
we have some--

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:50.990
well, we have an x.

00:47:50.990 --> 00:47:54.610
So we want to predict
the value of y given x.

00:47:54.610 --> 00:47:57.790
We want to fit here just
a line to this data.

00:47:57.790 --> 00:48:01.050
So we have two parameters
that we want to fit.

00:48:01.050 --> 00:48:03.210
So the intercept and the slope.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:07.010
Say that we have only one
observation from our ground

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:11.530
truth process, which is
the dashed line over here.

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:15.650
If we fit our linear model
to this data, of course,

00:48:15.650 --> 00:48:17.150
there could be many
possible-- well,

00:48:17.150 --> 00:48:19.210
in principle,
infinite lines that

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:22.530
pass through the point,
which in practice would mean

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:26.690
we would have a very broad
uncertainty over possible values

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:29.770
of our parameters represented
by this plot over here.

00:48:29.770 --> 00:48:31.830
So here we're plotting
the two parameters,

00:48:31.830 --> 00:48:33.850
say the intercept and the slope.

00:48:33.850 --> 00:48:37.010
And so we could see that while
the ground truth parameter is

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:40.490
represented here
by the red cross,

00:48:40.490 --> 00:48:44.187
our posterior distribution
is still pretty broad.

00:48:44.187 --> 00:48:45.520
What does this mean in practice?

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:50.600
Is that if we were to sample,
n, realizations of my posterior

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:52.720
distribution and
plot the line, you

00:48:52.720 --> 00:48:55.080
would get these
different red curves.

00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:57.520
If we would then
average out according

00:48:57.520 --> 00:48:59.460
to the predictive
posterior distribution,

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:01.160
we would get these
confidence bounds

00:49:01.160 --> 00:49:03.640
that we have on the right.

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:05.620
Are the plots clear?

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:12.760
Yeah.

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:15.920
And so what we're showing
here is basically progression

00:49:15.920 --> 00:49:17.580
as we collect more data.

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:19.600
So here we were
looking at the case

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:21.640
where we had only
one data point.

00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:23.180
Here we have more data points.

00:49:23.180 --> 00:49:25.680
And so our posterior
distribution over parameters

00:49:25.680 --> 00:49:28.520
is more narrow and closer
to the ground truth.

00:49:28.520 --> 00:49:30.360
And you see how the
overall uncertainty

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:32.320
bounds that come from
the posterior predictive

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:36.360
are also more
concentrated, until here we

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:40.040
are observing enough data to
have a pretty reliable estimate

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:41.920
of the ground truth model.

00:49:51.820 --> 00:49:56.700
And so given that this
is clear, let's briefly

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.800
mention a couple
of, I would say,

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:02.340
the most popular approaches
for modeling dynamical

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:05.880
systems in a way that we are
accounting for this uncertainty,

00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:07.320
and we'll be looking at two.

00:50:07.320 --> 00:50:09.500
The first one is
Gaussian processes,

00:50:09.500 --> 00:50:14.380
and the second one is ensembles.

00:50:14.380 --> 00:50:17.380
So a Gaussian Process
loosely speaking

00:50:17.380 --> 00:50:21.700
is-- basically represents a
distribution over functions.

00:50:21.700 --> 00:50:26.340
So you can imagine
that if we are sampling

00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:29.060
from a prior distribution
or functions, that

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:32.460
is before observing any
data, we will be getting

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.980
pretty different behavior.

00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:38.260
On the other hand, Gaussian
processes basically

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:41.290
formalize analytically
our mechanism

00:50:41.290 --> 00:50:44.330
to go from a prior
distribution over functions

00:50:44.330 --> 00:50:46.570
to a posterior
distribution over function

00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:50.915
in light of observed data,
which would basically be still

00:50:50.915 --> 00:50:52.290
a distribution
over function that

00:50:52.290 --> 00:50:54.790
is consistent with
the data at hand.

00:50:59.770 --> 00:51:02.550
When I first approach
also Gaussian processes,

00:51:02.550 --> 00:51:05.050
I would say one of the
most confusing aspects

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:07.930
to me was this idea of
distribution over functions

00:51:07.930 --> 00:51:09.450
and what actually meant.

00:51:09.450 --> 00:51:13.170
In practice, Gaussian
processes model data

00:51:13.170 --> 00:51:14.830
as multivariate Gaussians.

00:51:14.830 --> 00:51:18.330
So this is why they're referred
to as Gaussian processes.

00:51:18.330 --> 00:51:25.190
And basically encode this idea
that n-dimensional Gaussian,

00:51:25.190 --> 00:51:30.155
for example a
two-dimensional Gaussian

00:51:30.155 --> 00:51:34.510
can be mapped-- so a sample
from n-dimensional Gaussian

00:51:34.510 --> 00:51:38.190
can be mapped to basically an
approximation of a function.

00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:41.530
So say that we are sampling from
this two-dimensional Gaussian,

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:48.350
say this point over here,
which could be 5 and 4.

00:51:48.350 --> 00:51:52.510
Now, we could take this
two-dimensional basically a

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:57.310
vector, which we just sampled,
and if we plot it still

00:51:57.310 --> 00:51:59.990
in two dimensions,
what we could get

00:51:59.990 --> 00:52:06.150
is that we could get a first
value of 5, a second value of 4.

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:07.910
And by connecting
this, we're basically

00:52:07.910 --> 00:52:13.230
getting a discretization of
a function now described only

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:15.110
in this small interval.

00:52:15.110 --> 00:52:17.730
Now, of course, we can only
plot things in two dimensions.

00:52:17.730 --> 00:52:20.570
So maybe this might not yet
look like a function to you.

00:52:20.570 --> 00:52:23.470
But now imagine that we're
sampling an n-dimensional vector

00:52:23.470 --> 00:52:26.270
from a multivariate Gaussian.

00:52:26.270 --> 00:52:29.790
Here we would get just a
vector with more numbers.

00:52:29.790 --> 00:52:31.950
And if we plot them
in the same way,

00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:35.470
we would basically
get the expression--

00:52:35.470 --> 00:52:37.820
the approximation of a function.

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:47.380
So this is usually how these
are implemented in practice

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:51.180
and why people refer to these
as distribution over functions.

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:53.780
In practice, you can imagine
that Gaussian processes

00:52:53.780 --> 00:52:57.140
essentially provide
a mechanism to update

00:52:57.140 --> 00:53:01.780
from a prior distribution
over functions to a posterior

00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:03.880
distributions once we
have observed data.

00:53:08.740 --> 00:53:10.520
Again, we're sticking
to intuition,

00:53:10.520 --> 00:53:12.080
but I hope this is making sense.

00:53:12.080 --> 00:53:13.580
Yeah.

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:24.860
So here I was trying
to provide an intuitive

00:53:24.860 --> 00:53:27.940
understanding of what
people refer to as

00:53:27.940 --> 00:53:29.620
distribution over functions.

00:53:29.620 --> 00:53:32.100
So a Gaussian process
is essentially

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:36.070
representing multivariate
Gaussian distribution.

00:53:36.070 --> 00:53:39.210
So for example here,
I was representing it

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:40.850
in two-dimensional.

00:53:40.850 --> 00:53:43.850
So if we have this
two-dimensional Gaussian,

00:53:43.850 --> 00:53:45.890
a sample from this
two-dimensional Gaussian

00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:48.410
could just be this
point over here.

00:53:48.410 --> 00:53:52.450
Here I was imagining
4 or 5 as coordinates.

00:53:52.450 --> 00:53:59.590
If we would be wanted to plot
this point as a function,

00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:02.290
essentially, this
would essentially

00:54:02.290 --> 00:54:05.490
be something like this,
where we are basically

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.550
mapping this point and these
coordinates of this point,

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:12.250
but just unfolding it into a
function, if that makes sense.

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:15.210
Now, the intuition
is that what happens

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:19.930
if I want to build a full
continuous function as we were

00:54:19.930 --> 00:54:22.450
doing before, you could
imagine that it's basically

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:25.650
a more finely discretized
version of this

00:54:25.650 --> 00:54:27.730
with an n-dimensional vector.

00:54:27.730 --> 00:54:30.870
So rather than having
two-dimensional Gaussians,

00:54:30.870 --> 00:54:34.750
I would have an n-dimensional
Gaussian, say 100,

00:54:34.750 --> 00:54:37.310
then we would be able
to basically represent

00:54:37.310 --> 00:54:40.430
points on a very fine grid.

00:54:40.430 --> 00:54:42.330
And by basically
plotting these points,

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:44.470
you would get a function out.

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.630
Yeah.

00:54:46.630 --> 00:54:47.170
Yeah.

00:54:47.170 --> 00:54:50.580
So is it kind of
like [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:52.710
the values of the function?

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:55.510
Or is it like--

00:54:55.510 --> 00:54:57.430
does it have to be a
sequence of numbers

00:54:57.430 --> 00:54:59.610
that the function would output?

00:54:59.610 --> 00:55:00.110
Yeah.

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:01.870
So maybe to rephrase
what you're saying.

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:05.690
Here we are implicitly assuming
an order of the dimensions.

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:07.210
Yeah.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.710
But from the
perspective of learning

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:10.870
this doesn't really
change anything

00:55:10.870 --> 00:55:15.350
because we are still fitting the
Gaussian that we are learning

00:55:15.350 --> 00:55:17.050
to a high-dimensional object.

00:55:17.050 --> 00:55:21.890
So for us, data will be living
in this high-dimensional space.

00:55:21.890 --> 00:55:24.390
And we're just fitting
a n-dimensional Gaussian

00:55:24.390 --> 00:55:26.190
to that data.

00:55:26.190 --> 00:55:28.970
This is just the way
we would then plot it.

00:55:28.970 --> 00:55:29.470
Yeah.

00:55:35.120 --> 00:55:38.820
So why are Gaussian
processes popular?

00:55:38.820 --> 00:55:43.860
First of all, Gaussian processes
are extremely data efficient,

00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:47.700
and they provide an
analytical expression

00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:51.900
for the exact posterior,
that is the exact posterior

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:54.980
over functions
described by your data.

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:58.700
Meaning, given some data
points and some assumptions

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:03.420
on Gaussianity,
this distribution

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:05.140
is actually the
exact representation

00:56:05.140 --> 00:56:07.340
of the posterior
distribution over functions

00:56:07.340 --> 00:56:09.060
that explain this data.

00:56:09.060 --> 00:56:14.140
This is pretty cool, because if
we wanted to achieve something

00:56:14.140 --> 00:56:16.760
similar with neural networks,
this would be very hard,

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:19.940
especially with
this amount of data.

00:56:19.940 --> 00:56:22.360
Because this is an
extremely low data regime,

00:56:22.360 --> 00:56:26.380
and so getting these extremely
well-behaved confidence bounds

00:56:26.380 --> 00:56:28.180
is very challenging to have.

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:33.570
On the other hand, Gaussian
processes achieve this

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:38.970
by having an exact
representation of the posterior.

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:43.010
On the other hand, I mentioned
analytical expression

00:56:43.010 --> 00:56:44.330
of the posterior.

00:56:44.330 --> 00:56:48.970
This analytical expression,
at least in the nominal case,

00:56:48.970 --> 00:56:51.870
entails having to do
some matrix inversions.

00:56:51.870 --> 00:56:54.730
And so depending which
as a function of n, where

00:56:54.730 --> 00:56:57.090
n is the number of data
sets of data points,

00:56:57.090 --> 00:56:59.850
and so scaling
Gaussian processes

00:56:59.850 --> 00:57:04.230
to high-dimensional data
is actually complex.

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:06.290
And so there are extensions
of Gaussian processes

00:57:06.290 --> 00:57:07.310
that try to do so.

00:57:07.310 --> 00:57:11.190
But at least in
their face value,

00:57:11.190 --> 00:57:12.830
this is definitely a weakness.

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:20.690
A second approach for
estimating uncertainty

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:23.850
is referred to as
bootstrap ensembles.

00:57:23.850 --> 00:57:26.370
And the idea is that--

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:28.190
so we have this
general mechanism

00:57:28.190 --> 00:57:33.230
for learning dynamical models
through supervised learning.

00:57:33.230 --> 00:57:37.990
That is, we just fit a
black box model to our data,

00:57:37.990 --> 00:57:43.750
and we want to have an
encode an idea of uncertainty

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:45.750
in this estimation problem.

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:49.350
So what we can do is that
the idea of ensembles

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:54.630
is let's train multiple models
and see if they agree and where.

00:57:54.630 --> 00:57:58.430
With the underlying idea that
these different models will--

00:57:58.430 --> 00:58:00.550
so different models
trained independently

00:58:00.550 --> 00:58:03.190
to fit the same data
will likely agree

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:05.630
when they are certain
about their predictions,

00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:08.990
and they will likely disagree
whenever there is uncertainty.

00:58:08.990 --> 00:58:12.670
So say you have n versions and
copies of the models that have

00:58:12.670 --> 00:58:15.270
been trained independently.

00:58:15.270 --> 00:58:17.230
If they will be
probably agreeing

00:58:17.230 --> 00:58:19.630
close to the regime
of observed data,

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:21.590
and they will potentially
disagree when we are

00:58:21.590 --> 00:58:25.310
going far away from our data.

00:58:25.310 --> 00:58:30.860
And so ensembles basically
instantiate this idea

00:58:30.860 --> 00:58:35.053
through an algorithm, which
is basically saying nothing

00:58:35.053 --> 00:58:36.220
else that we're saying here.

00:58:36.220 --> 00:58:38.980
In ensembles, you would just
train independently and copies

00:58:38.980 --> 00:58:42.540
of your model, and then
average the prediction

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:44.440
according to these n models.

00:58:47.500 --> 00:58:49.900
So specifically, what
you would be doing is

00:58:49.900 --> 00:58:53.740
we say we have five
different neural network

00:58:53.740 --> 00:58:56.100
models, which we train.

00:58:56.100 --> 00:59:01.060
We could then get five different
predictions for our next state.

00:59:01.060 --> 00:59:03.060
And so what we can
then do is maybe

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:05.220
our prediction would be
the average prediction

00:59:05.220 --> 00:59:06.700
across these models.

00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:07.840
This could be one idea.

00:59:15.820 --> 00:59:20.020
And maybe another way
to visualize this is

00:59:20.020 --> 00:59:23.420
say that we have-- so here the
x-axis represents the parameter

00:59:23.420 --> 00:59:29.960
space theta, and the y-axis
represents our-- basically

00:59:29.960 --> 00:59:31.880
this is the posterior
distribution

00:59:31.880 --> 00:59:34.080
over parameters given data.

00:59:34.080 --> 00:59:36.320
So say that the ground
truth posterior distribution

00:59:36.320 --> 00:59:42.600
is represented by the shaded
multimodal distribution here.

00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:46.680
And for now disregard
VI, which stands

00:59:46.680 --> 00:59:50.600
for variational inference,
and this multi-SWAG algorithm.

00:59:50.600 --> 00:59:54.680
The ensembles would
essentially mean, in this case,

00:59:54.680 --> 01:00:00.600
fitting for different models,
the blue ones, independently.

01:00:00.600 --> 01:00:03.400
Through optimization,
these four models

01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:07.560
will converge to for different
values of the parameters theta.

01:00:07.560 --> 01:00:10.080
And so these
parameters theta might

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:12.120
converge to different
local minima,

01:00:12.120 --> 01:00:14.400
depending on how the
optimization goes.

01:00:14.400 --> 01:00:17.560
And so these are essentially
an empirical approximation

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:20.560
of the ground truth
posterior distribution.

01:00:20.560 --> 01:00:24.270
Because they will naturally--
because of the stochasticity

01:00:24.270 --> 01:00:28.450
of stochastic gradient descent
and the optimization process,

01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:31.670
they will converge to
different local minima.

01:00:31.670 --> 01:00:35.070
And so these are a way
of empirically exploring

01:00:35.070 --> 01:00:38.910
the landscape and approximating
the ground truth posterior

01:00:38.910 --> 01:00:39.730
distribution.

01:00:42.990 --> 01:00:44.310
Yeah.

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:49.990
Are you saying the differences
between the different ones

01:00:49.990 --> 01:00:54.070
because the goal--

01:00:54.070 --> 01:00:57.470
here you have a multimodal
distribution over theta,

01:00:57.470 --> 01:01:02.570
your goal is to figure out what
all the modes are by having--

01:01:02.570 --> 01:01:06.150
these each one of these
you said neural networks.

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:10.510
Each one of these represent
a different mode that you

01:01:10.510 --> 01:01:12.430
hopefully get [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:12.430 --> 01:01:13.290
Yeah, indeed.

01:01:13.290 --> 01:01:17.670
So the idea here is that this
learning problem, very likely

01:01:17.670 --> 01:01:20.890
has a highly multimodal
landscape of solutions.

01:01:23.420 --> 01:01:25.640
The approaches we were
discussing before,

01:01:25.640 --> 01:01:30.220
like Gaussian processes, try to
derive analytical expressions

01:01:30.220 --> 01:01:32.540
for this exact distributions.

01:01:32.540 --> 01:01:35.260
Ensembles here,
on the other hand,

01:01:35.260 --> 01:01:38.780
disregard any of
this formal approach

01:01:38.780 --> 01:01:43.500
and just decide to instantiate
n copies of these models,

01:01:43.500 --> 01:01:47.020
maybe initialize
them differently.

01:01:47.020 --> 01:01:48.700
The stochasticity
of gradient descent

01:01:48.700 --> 01:01:54.740
still will lead them to converge
to different final solutions.

01:01:54.740 --> 01:01:57.420
And by doing so, the
hope is that they

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:01.620
will cover meaningful different
modes of the ground truth

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:04.525
posterior distribution.

01:02:04.525 --> 01:02:05.900
So I think your
original question

01:02:05.900 --> 01:02:10.460
is, how do you differentiate
between these copies?

01:02:10.460 --> 01:02:12.860
I would say, if you're
talking about neural networks

01:02:12.860 --> 01:02:16.640
specifically, typically these
models are initialized randomly.

01:02:16.640 --> 01:02:19.980
So the initial values of
the parameters are random.

01:02:19.980 --> 01:02:23.360
And also we're optimizing them
to stochastic gradient descent.

01:02:23.360 --> 01:02:25.360
So even the directions
that they're following

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:27.500
will be random to some extent.

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.920
So there is some natural
stochasticity that comes.

01:02:29.920 --> 01:02:30.800
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:02:37.700 --> 01:02:39.360
Yes.

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:42.540
And then also, why
Dirac distributions?

01:02:42.540 --> 01:02:44.960
This might just be because
I forgot [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:45.820
Yeah, no, indeed.

01:02:45.820 --> 01:02:48.260
So this is something
that I did not discuss.

01:02:48.260 --> 01:02:50.997
So the idea is that
formally-- so I

01:02:50.997 --> 01:02:53.580
think, hopefully in practice,
it makes sense what we're doing.

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:55.220
So we're initializing
for models,

01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:59.620
and then we have for different
converge parameters theta,

01:02:59.620 --> 01:03:01.720
and then we could
average over them.

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:05.680
What are we doing in practice
is that we are approximating

01:03:05.680 --> 01:03:09.560
the ground truth posterior
distribution by taking

01:03:09.560 --> 01:03:13.280
the average of what
is referred to as

01:03:13.280 --> 01:03:17.480
a Dirac distribution, which
is essentially saying that--

01:03:17.480 --> 01:03:22.190
say that I have one
observation of my theta.

01:03:22.190 --> 01:03:24.910
The Dirac distribution is
something that is basically

01:03:24.910 --> 01:03:27.790
a spiky distribution that
gives all the probability mass

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:30.230
exactly to that point.

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:35.390
And so this is, if you
wish more formally,

01:03:35.390 --> 01:03:37.790
how this process of
averaging out predictions

01:03:37.790 --> 01:03:39.750
from different models
would translate

01:03:39.750 --> 01:03:44.030
to the predictive posterior
that we were describing before

01:03:44.030 --> 01:03:46.210
through the lens of these
Dirac distributions.

01:03:53.270 --> 01:03:55.550
So remember that in the
predictive posterior, what

01:03:55.550 --> 01:03:57.790
we are saying is that
we want to average out

01:03:57.790 --> 01:04:00.830
over all the different
models that we're training

01:04:00.830 --> 01:04:03.910
or specifically average
out over the posterior

01:04:03.910 --> 01:04:07.150
distribution of our parameters.

01:04:07.150 --> 01:04:08.990
So do you remember
the discussion

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:12.290
we were doing on predictive
posterior and what that means?

01:04:12.290 --> 01:04:14.870
How do we compute those
confidence bounds?

01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:18.100
Is that we were basically--

01:04:18.100 --> 01:04:20.400
for each aim in
the discrete case,

01:04:20.400 --> 01:04:22.500
for each value of
our parameters,

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:25.620
we were basically
predicting with that value

01:04:25.620 --> 01:04:29.820
of the parameters, and then
weighting that probability--

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:33.820
that prediction with the
probability of how certain

01:04:33.820 --> 01:04:37.060
we were of that specific
value of the parameters.

01:04:37.060 --> 01:04:41.760
In this case, we are doing so
not in the continuous case,

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:46.780
but through these
Dirac approximations.

01:04:46.780 --> 01:04:47.760
Does that make sense?

01:04:57.580 --> 01:05:02.100
But so long story short,
what we've been doing so far

01:05:02.100 --> 01:05:05.780
is that we introduced
this basic recipe

01:05:05.780 --> 01:05:08.940
for model-based
reinforcement learning,

01:05:08.940 --> 01:05:14.320
namely some version of run
a policy in the environment,

01:05:14.320 --> 01:05:18.120
collect transitions, fit the
model to those transitions,

01:05:18.120 --> 01:05:20.160
and then use that
model for planning.

01:05:20.160 --> 01:05:24.480
We motivated how uncertainty, or
at least estimating uncertainty

01:05:24.480 --> 01:05:27.880
on those predictions is
likely very important.

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:31.000
And now let's look at
some specific examples

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:34.840
and how we could make
this work in practice.

01:05:34.840 --> 01:05:42.820
So how can we make use of
this additional knowledge

01:05:42.820 --> 01:05:45.000
that we just gained?

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:48.920
Specifically, one mechanism to
do so could be the following.

01:05:48.920 --> 01:05:52.360
So let's assume that we have a
candidate action sequence again,

01:05:52.360 --> 01:05:56.560
some given to us by
some planning algorithm.

01:05:56.560 --> 01:05:59.480
And now we have
approximate model

01:05:59.480 --> 01:06:02.640
of the posterior distribution
of the parameters theta, which

01:06:02.640 --> 01:06:05.560
we learned through, for
example, one of the methods

01:06:05.560 --> 01:06:09.040
we discussed today.

01:06:09.040 --> 01:06:14.030
So we could sample a specific
value of our parameters theta

01:06:14.030 --> 01:06:16.870
from this posterior
distribution.

01:06:16.870 --> 01:06:19.510
And we could now propagate
the learned dynamics

01:06:19.510 --> 01:06:22.590
through this specific
instantiation

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:24.010
of the dynamical model.

01:06:28.270 --> 01:06:31.830
We could then use this
propagated trajectory

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:34.350
to compute a predicted reward.

01:06:34.350 --> 01:06:37.050
So we're basically simulating
in our head what is happening.

01:06:37.050 --> 01:06:39.590
We are given a candidate
action sequence.

01:06:39.590 --> 01:06:42.150
We are sampling
one of the models

01:06:42.150 --> 01:06:44.910
from our posterior
distribution over models.

01:06:44.910 --> 01:06:46.710
And we're using this
to propagate forward

01:06:46.710 --> 01:06:48.870
what is happening, and
scoring the quality of what

01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:52.590
is happening.

01:06:52.590 --> 01:06:56.510
And then we can just
repeat these three steps

01:06:56.510 --> 01:06:59.990
to basically compute
the average reward

01:06:59.990 --> 01:07:06.070
according to our learned
posterior distribution.

01:07:06.070 --> 01:07:11.630
So specifically, we would be--
so what this algorithm is doing

01:07:11.630 --> 01:07:15.340
is directly instantiating this
idea that we were describing

01:07:15.340 --> 01:07:17.020
pictorially over here.

01:07:17.020 --> 01:07:20.180
So in the plot there, we are
saying that uncertainty gives us

01:07:20.180 --> 01:07:24.980
this mechanism through which we
can compute the expected reward

01:07:24.980 --> 01:07:26.820
under our model.

01:07:26.820 --> 01:07:30.900
And so in this way,
we could basically

01:07:30.900 --> 01:07:35.660
approximate the expected reward
under all of our predicted

01:07:35.660 --> 01:07:36.440
samples.

01:07:39.165 --> 01:07:40.040
Does that make sense?

01:07:40.040 --> 01:07:41.873
Maybe, again, more
visually what we're doing

01:07:41.873 --> 01:07:49.220
is that we are given a
candidate action sequence.

01:07:49.220 --> 01:07:53.780
We sample maybe one
version or one parameters

01:07:53.780 --> 01:07:57.500
from our posterior distribution
and we propagate the model

01:07:57.500 --> 01:07:58.740
forward.

01:07:58.740 --> 01:08:02.420
Here we would be
reaching a specific sum

01:08:02.420 --> 01:08:07.820
of rewards over time according
to this specific model.

01:08:07.820 --> 01:08:11.160
We could then sample a
different version of that model,

01:08:11.160 --> 01:08:13.720
and we could have a different
propagated trajectory,

01:08:13.720 --> 01:08:15.980
with a different reward, right?

01:08:18.840 --> 01:08:21.399
If we repeat this
multiple times,

01:08:21.399 --> 01:08:25.439
we would then have n of
these propagated futures.

01:08:25.439 --> 01:08:28.660
And so what we could do is
that if we average them out,

01:08:28.660 --> 01:08:31.520
we would then be
computing an approximation

01:08:31.520 --> 01:08:35.260
of the expected reward under
the uncertainty of our model.

01:08:46.319 --> 01:08:50.920
So of course, one
thing that I really

01:08:50.920 --> 01:08:55.080
want to make sure to convey
is that this is obviously

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:55.880
just a choice.

01:08:55.880 --> 01:09:00.319
So we're not takeaway way
here, I think should not be--

01:09:00.319 --> 01:09:02.878
this is the best way
to go for when you're

01:09:02.878 --> 01:09:04.920
talking about model-based
reinforcement learning,

01:09:04.920 --> 01:09:07.740
because ultimately, it depends
on the problem at hand.

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:09.750
So maybe there is
even no need at all

01:09:09.750 --> 01:09:11.770
to look at uncertainty
quantification,

01:09:11.770 --> 01:09:15.630
because just having your
linear time invariant model

01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:18.189
is well-estimated
enough and so there

01:09:18.189 --> 01:09:20.510
is no need to do any of this.

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:25.870
But in the general case where
system dynamic approximation is

01:09:25.870 --> 01:09:29.430
challenging and you want
to use high capacity

01:09:29.430 --> 01:09:32.510
models for approximating
our dynamics,

01:09:32.510 --> 01:09:34.350
uncertainty plays a big role.

01:09:34.350 --> 01:09:36.430
And so this could
be one instantiation

01:09:36.430 --> 01:09:40.310
of this idea of let's
use whatever favorite way

01:09:40.310 --> 01:09:44.109
to estimate a posterior
distribution over parameters

01:09:44.109 --> 01:09:48.229
of our model, and then use
this to meaningfully represent

01:09:48.229 --> 01:09:50.689
expectation under
the uncertainty.

01:09:55.510 --> 01:09:59.230
And so just to repeat myself,
this is only a choice.

01:09:59.230 --> 01:10:02.250
But this is one instantiation
of this more general idea.

01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:05.990
That is, whenever we want to
plan through a learned model,

01:10:05.990 --> 01:10:07.930
we might want to be--

01:10:07.930 --> 01:10:11.050
we might be interested in
evaluating the expected reward

01:10:11.050 --> 01:10:12.390
under the learned model.

01:10:16.810 --> 01:10:22.050
So a particularly impactful
paper, now a few years old,

01:10:22.050 --> 01:10:26.330
but that uses exactly these
ideas, introduces algorithm

01:10:26.330 --> 01:10:28.450
called PETS.

01:10:28.450 --> 01:10:30.370
Whereby the main
idea is that they

01:10:30.370 --> 01:10:34.090
were using as a model, basically
an ensemble of neural networks

01:10:34.090 --> 01:10:38.970
to approximate the
posterior over the model.

01:10:38.970 --> 01:10:44.570
They were then using a sample,
basically different models

01:10:44.570 --> 01:10:47.170
from this ensemble, and use
them to generate predictions

01:10:47.170 --> 01:10:49.170
of different features,
exactly as we were

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:53.010
saying in the previous slide.

01:10:53.010 --> 01:10:57.090
And then they
would be using this

01:10:57.090 --> 01:10:59.470
in our model predictive
control fashion.

01:10:59.470 --> 01:11:03.410
So they would basically
have a cross-entropy method

01:11:03.410 --> 01:11:09.000
to generate this
candidate plans.

01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:12.440
Through the exact
same algorithm,

01:11:12.440 --> 01:11:14.600
which we just described,
they will basically

01:11:14.600 --> 01:11:16.520
take these candidate
plans and score them

01:11:16.520 --> 01:11:20.280
according to samples from these
ensemble of neural networks,

01:11:20.280 --> 01:11:23.120
and then pick the best
action based on that,

01:11:23.120 --> 01:11:26.100
and implement this in a
receding horizon fashion.

01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:29.280
So you predict
the full plan, you

01:11:29.280 --> 01:11:31.600
actuate only the first
step in the plan,

01:11:31.600 --> 01:11:34.760
and you repeat this process.

01:11:34.760 --> 01:11:36.218
And so this is--
just a quick note,

01:11:36.218 --> 01:11:37.718
but it's something
that I think it's

01:11:37.718 --> 01:11:39.800
a-- this is a nice paper
if you want to read

01:11:39.800 --> 01:11:41.620
about these models in practice.

01:11:46.720 --> 01:11:49.640
Does it make sense how
this is one instantiation

01:11:49.640 --> 01:11:50.780
of what we're saying?

01:11:55.280 --> 01:11:58.720
So in this paper specifically,
and I'm using this

01:11:58.720 --> 01:12:01.680
as an example to highlight
the classical benefits

01:12:01.680 --> 01:12:05.350
of model-based RL,
is that here they

01:12:05.350 --> 01:12:09.790
would be measuring performance
of-- well, their method in blue,

01:12:09.790 --> 01:12:12.690
compared to a bunch of
different baselines.

01:12:12.690 --> 01:12:14.230
But for example,
they're comparing

01:12:14.230 --> 01:12:18.070
against PPO or soft
actor-critic, which is also

01:12:18.070 --> 01:12:20.230
a version of the actor-critic
methods we discussed

01:12:20.230 --> 01:12:24.190
in class, across a number
of different tasks.

01:12:24.190 --> 01:12:27.550
And I would say the most
interesting result here

01:12:27.550 --> 01:12:31.790
is that these model-based
methods, namely the blue curves,

01:12:31.790 --> 01:12:34.690
generally are much
more sample efficient.

01:12:34.690 --> 01:12:37.030
So if you compare
the performance

01:12:37.030 --> 01:12:39.790
on the y-axis in
general, with respect

01:12:39.790 --> 01:12:42.310
to the number of time
steps through which they

01:12:42.310 --> 01:12:44.370
had to interact with
the environment,

01:12:44.370 --> 01:12:47.190
you could see that maybe the
performance at convergence

01:12:47.190 --> 01:12:49.230
is fairly similar.

01:12:49.230 --> 01:12:54.470
But the blue curves typically
tend to converge to this--

01:12:54.470 --> 01:12:56.710
converge performance
much faster.

01:12:56.710 --> 01:13:01.110
And this is because through
learning the model itself,

01:13:01.110 --> 01:13:04.370
we can basically circumvent
a lot of the interactions

01:13:04.370 --> 01:13:05.830
with the environment.

01:13:09.010 --> 01:13:12.930
So generally, the
benefits in model-based RL

01:13:12.930 --> 01:13:15.590
often relate with
sample efficiency.

01:13:25.610 --> 01:13:30.310
So any questions before
wrapping up the class?

01:13:36.090 --> 01:13:36.810
Yeah.

01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:38.110
What's your favorite method?

01:13:38.110 --> 01:13:40.570
[LAUGHTER]

01:13:40.570 --> 01:13:48.890
So I like to be fairly agnostic
and pragmatic, depending

01:13:48.890 --> 01:13:50.810
on the problem at hand.

01:13:50.810 --> 01:13:53.202
I would say that--

01:13:53.202 --> 01:13:57.810
[LAUGHS] you're saying this
is the diplomatic answer.

01:13:57.810 --> 01:14:00.770
So I would say that the
model-free algorithms are

01:14:00.770 --> 01:14:03.540
definitely more mature
as a technology,

01:14:03.540 --> 01:14:05.360
and so I would say
algorithms such as PPO

01:14:05.360 --> 01:14:07.320
and soft actor-critic
are typically the way

01:14:07.320 --> 01:14:10.120
to go for continuous control.

01:14:10.120 --> 01:14:13.440
Model-based RL is still very
active line of research.

01:14:13.440 --> 01:14:16.000
So if I have to
pick one, I would go

01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:18.020
for a soft actor-critic or PPO.

01:14:18.020 --> 01:14:18.520
Yeah.

01:14:18.520 --> 01:14:21.140
Well, those are
two, but those two.

01:14:21.140 --> 01:14:23.600
Yeah.

01:14:23.600 --> 01:14:26.320
So I think this is a
nicely way into-- basically

01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:28.680
what I wanted to discuss here.

01:14:28.680 --> 01:14:30.640
Because throughout
this class, we

01:14:30.640 --> 01:14:35.840
have been introducing n
different ways of behaving when

01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:37.180
looking at optimal control.

01:14:37.180 --> 01:14:39.740
So ranging from
open loop methods,

01:14:39.740 --> 01:14:42.160
it could be indirect and
direct, closed-loop, dynamic

01:14:42.160 --> 01:14:45.160
programming, reinforcement
learning, imitation learning.

01:14:45.160 --> 01:14:47.295
And so the question
that you could ask is,

01:14:47.295 --> 01:14:49.420
I mean this is great, but
you're just confusing me.

01:14:49.420 --> 01:14:52.380
Now I have to pick among
hundreds of these algorithms,

01:14:52.380 --> 01:14:54.120
how do I navigate this?

01:14:54.120 --> 01:14:59.240
And the point here is
that these methods are not

01:14:59.240 --> 01:15:03.370
in competition with each other,
but they're actually-- well,

01:15:03.370 --> 01:15:04.830
different methods
will be tailored

01:15:04.830 --> 01:15:06.148
to different applications.

01:15:06.148 --> 01:15:08.190
And actually most of the
time, it's these methods

01:15:08.190 --> 01:15:10.270
are combined with each other.

01:15:10.270 --> 01:15:16.270
And this is because the
decision-making pipeline

01:15:16.270 --> 01:15:18.430
is typically
organized as through

01:15:18.430 --> 01:15:21.550
a hierarchical structure.

01:15:21.550 --> 01:15:23.870
Specifically, if we
were to decompose

01:15:23.870 --> 01:15:26.630
this hierarchical
structure, generally, we

01:15:26.630 --> 01:15:30.190
would start with having a model
of the world or a representation

01:15:30.190 --> 01:15:32.750
of what is interesting for us.

01:15:32.750 --> 01:15:37.150
For example, take the case
of autonomous driving.

01:15:37.150 --> 01:15:39.230
We might have some
maybe semantic maps

01:15:39.230 --> 01:15:42.590
of what we are observing
in our environment.

01:15:42.590 --> 01:15:45.070
And given whatever
representation of the world

01:15:45.070 --> 01:15:46.910
we are interested
in, we would then

01:15:46.910 --> 01:15:50.550
have the autonomy
stack is typically

01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:52.750
decomposed through
different modules that

01:15:52.750 --> 01:15:54.230
interact with each other.

01:15:54.230 --> 01:15:57.190
So the first kind of decision
that you have to make

01:15:57.190 --> 01:16:00.180
is typically some higher
level goal for our system.

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:01.930
So we are perceiving
the world, and now we

01:16:01.930 --> 01:16:05.170
have to choose what
we want to perform.

01:16:05.170 --> 01:16:07.290
Specifically, these
high-level decisions

01:16:07.290 --> 01:16:10.950
might be continuing the
autonomous driving example,

01:16:10.950 --> 01:16:13.050
something along the
lines of picking if I

01:16:13.050 --> 01:16:14.550
want to change a lane or not.

01:16:14.550 --> 01:16:18.450
So these very high-level
actions and decisions

01:16:18.450 --> 01:16:19.890
that we want to make.

01:16:19.890 --> 01:16:22.290
At this level of
the hierarchy, this

01:16:22.290 --> 01:16:24.090
is typically where
methods that belong

01:16:24.090 --> 01:16:28.410
to closed-loop dynamic
programming like algorithms lie.

01:16:28.410 --> 01:16:30.170
And this is because
for these higher

01:16:30.170 --> 01:16:33.530
level decision-making
aspects, we typically

01:16:33.530 --> 01:16:36.290
want to encode stochasticity
and want to make sure

01:16:36.290 --> 01:16:38.970
that we are dealing
with this stochasticity

01:16:38.970 --> 01:16:41.410
when picking decisions.

01:16:41.410 --> 01:16:45.590
And the output of this is
typically in a form of a goal,

01:16:45.590 --> 01:16:48.610
for example, lane change.

01:16:48.610 --> 01:16:51.690
Now we have this goal, but we
have to make it actionable.

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:53.890
So we want to make
this such that we

01:16:53.890 --> 01:16:59.520
have an actual trajectory which
we can enact in the world.

01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:01.960
And so this is something
that usually is dealt

01:17:01.960 --> 01:17:03.640
with open-loop techniques.

01:17:03.640 --> 01:17:05.360
So we would have this
higher level goal,

01:17:05.360 --> 01:17:06.880
like a navigation goal.

01:17:06.880 --> 01:17:09.640
And then we want to translate
this navigation goal

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:14.360
into a finer grained trajectory,
which we want our car to follow.

01:17:14.360 --> 01:17:17.560
This trajectory obviously
encodes some notion

01:17:17.560 --> 01:17:20.400
of the dynamics of the
vehicle, despite this

01:17:20.400 --> 01:17:24.700
might be maybe some lower
fidelity models of the dynamics.

01:17:24.700 --> 01:17:27.920
But still we want to
encode some constraints

01:17:27.920 --> 01:17:30.320
in terms of safety, some
constraints in terms

01:17:30.320 --> 01:17:31.500
of dynamics.

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:37.240
And so this is where open-loop
techniques typically fall.

01:17:37.240 --> 01:17:39.240
Once we have a
trajectory, we want

01:17:39.240 --> 01:17:46.600
to make sure this is physically
tractable and implemented

01:17:46.600 --> 01:17:47.860
on the hardware.

01:17:47.860 --> 01:17:54.080
So this is where usually ideas
from model predictive control,

01:17:54.080 --> 01:17:58.180
specifically in its tracking
instantiations commonplace.

01:17:58.180 --> 01:18:00.500
So we would have a
reference trajectory

01:18:00.500 --> 01:18:03.648
that is given from the
open-loop algorithm.

01:18:03.648 --> 01:18:05.440
And now we want to make
sure, first of all,

01:18:05.440 --> 01:18:07.080
that we're tracking
this over time,

01:18:07.080 --> 01:18:11.820
but also potentially including
a finer grained dynamical models

01:18:11.820 --> 01:18:18.040
of the system, maybe some
safety-oriented constraints,

01:18:18.040 --> 01:18:24.340
which we were avoiding in the
open-loop stage, and so forth.

01:18:24.340 --> 01:18:27.780
So we start from
representation of the world.

01:18:27.780 --> 01:18:30.220
We use a higher
level decision-making

01:18:30.220 --> 01:18:33.020
to have a high-level
goal for our system.

01:18:33.020 --> 01:18:36.340
We compute a trajectory that
tries to achieve that goal,

01:18:36.340 --> 01:18:38.720
we then want to implement
this trajectory in practice,

01:18:38.720 --> 01:18:41.660
so we want to make sure
we are tracking it.

01:18:41.660 --> 01:18:43.900
And obviously last
but not least, we

01:18:43.900 --> 01:18:46.845
feed these to the actuators.

01:18:46.845 --> 01:18:48.220
So at the end of
the day, we have

01:18:48.220 --> 01:18:51.720
to make this work on
the real-world hardware.

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:55.250
And so we want to make sure that
this is actually tracked by what

01:18:55.250 --> 01:18:56.970
is happening in reality.

01:18:56.970 --> 01:19:00.090
And so this is usually where
things like PID controls

01:19:00.090 --> 01:19:02.970
come in place.

01:19:02.970 --> 01:19:05.250
Of course throughout
all of this,

01:19:05.250 --> 01:19:08.510
we might have some
safety concerns

01:19:08.510 --> 01:19:12.810
and we could be dealing with
safety critical applications.

01:19:12.810 --> 01:19:15.190
For example, in the
case of driving,

01:19:15.190 --> 01:19:19.250
we might have some set
of states which we do not

01:19:19.250 --> 01:19:22.090
want to visit because of
some safety concepts which

01:19:22.090 --> 01:19:23.250
we care about.

01:19:23.250 --> 01:19:28.610
And so the safety concepts which
may be computed through things

01:19:28.610 --> 01:19:30.970
such Hamilton-Jacobi
reachability

01:19:30.970 --> 01:19:32.530
are typically
mechanism through which

01:19:32.530 --> 01:19:37.890
we can inform both or either
or either of open-loop

01:19:37.890 --> 01:19:41.090
and the tracking module.

01:19:41.090 --> 01:19:45.170
So in a way, I hope this is
starting to make a little bit

01:19:45.170 --> 01:19:45.830
more concrete.

01:19:45.830 --> 01:19:48.490
What I mean when I say that
these techniques, which

01:19:48.490 --> 01:19:50.570
we introduced in this
class, are definitely not

01:19:50.570 --> 01:19:52.920
exclusive, but
actually typically

01:19:52.920 --> 01:19:56.800
combine in autonomy stack.

01:19:56.800 --> 01:19:58.440
And the details of
these algorithms

01:19:58.440 --> 01:20:00.980
will obviously depend
on the application.

01:20:00.980 --> 01:20:03.900
So we hope that with
this class, we gave you,

01:20:03.900 --> 01:20:06.120
first of all, a broad
understanding of all

01:20:06.120 --> 01:20:13.360
of these concepts, and also
focused on the higher level

01:20:13.360 --> 01:20:16.320
understanding of how these
modules could be combined,

01:20:16.320 --> 01:20:20.480
and the pros and
cons of these ideas.

01:20:20.480 --> 01:20:24.520
All of this is obviously in--

01:20:24.520 --> 01:20:28.080
we should, I think, concretize
this in a moment in time

01:20:28.080 --> 01:20:30.880
where learning-based and
end-to-end approaches

01:20:30.880 --> 01:20:33.720
are definitely becoming
more and more popular.

01:20:33.720 --> 01:20:36.520
And so I would say that
historically, learning

01:20:36.520 --> 01:20:40.020
has started from the higher
levels of this abstraction.

01:20:40.020 --> 01:20:44.400
So perception is
the first situation

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:48.400
where learning basically
became the de facto standard.

01:20:48.400 --> 01:20:52.980
And I would say that right
now, end-to-end approaches

01:20:52.980 --> 01:20:57.260
are more or less starting
to carve their way down

01:20:57.260 --> 01:20:58.360
along this hierarchy.

01:20:58.360 --> 01:21:01.320
I would say right now they are
basically somewhere over here.

01:21:01.320 --> 01:21:04.980
So in between the open-loop
and the closed-loop trajectory

01:21:04.980 --> 01:21:06.820
tracking stage.

01:21:06.820 --> 01:21:10.500
I would say even the most
bullish companies or approaches

01:21:10.500 --> 01:21:13.480
that claim end-to-end
approaches,

01:21:13.480 --> 01:21:17.820
typically they don't mess with
the lower level components.

01:21:17.820 --> 01:21:19.380
And this is because
here it's really

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:21.180
where you want to
make sure you have

01:21:21.180 --> 01:21:24.700
fine-grained control, your
encoding hard constraint

01:21:24.700 --> 01:21:28.340
guarantees, and making sure
that everything works concretely

01:21:28.340 --> 01:21:29.120
in practice.

01:21:32.860 --> 01:21:34.938
So if there are no
further questions,

01:21:34.938 --> 01:21:36.980
I want to really thank
you very much for sticking

01:21:36.980 --> 01:21:40.100
with us throughout this
quarter, and wishing you best

01:21:40.100 --> 01:21:41.240
of luck for the exam.

01:21:41.240 --> 01:21:44.590
[APPLAUSE]

